<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aschaffenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aschaffenburg is a city on the Main in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-franconia">Lower Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>. For centuries it was the second residence of the archbishop-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, who built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannisburg-palace">Johannisburg Palace</a> there in 1605–1614. After 1803 it was the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a>'s state, and it passed to Bavaria in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg.jpg" alt="Aschaffenburg" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rainer Lippert → Derivative work: Steffen Schmitz (Carschten) · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aschaffenburger_Schloss,_edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
